= The London Ultimatum

On 5 May the Allies send Berlin the bill for the war: 132 billion gold marks, to be accepted within six days. If Germany refuses, the Ruhr will be occupied. French troops have held Düsseldorf and Duisburg since March.

[? if e21_new_cabinet = 1 : Fehrenbach's cabinet resigns. On 10 May Joseph Wirth of the Centre forms a government with the SPD and the DDP, and the Reichstag accepts the ultimatum. Wirth calls it the policy of fulfilment. ?][? if kapp_outcome = 3 : The workers' government accepts the ultimatum with a promise that the rich will pay, and every party to its right calls it treason. ?][? if kapp_outcome = 2 : The generals' government blusters, and then accepts. ?] The presses of the Reichsbank are already running.

- @against: Vote against: the workers will pay for it.
- @real_values: Demand that the rich pay, in shares and land.

@against
subtitle: Clear, simple, and it changes nothing.
on-arrival: militancy += 2; lean_offensive += 1

Our deputies vote no. The Rote Fahne prints a cartoon of Wirth handing a worker's wage packet to a French general.

@real_values
subtitle: Slow to catch on. If the mark falls, the unions will remember who proposed it.
on-arrival: e21_real_values = 1; uspd_relation += 3; lean_unionist += 2

The party demands the seizure of real values: a fifth of the shares, land and buildings of the propertied classes, to pay the reparations instead of the printing press. The demand is read out at a few union meetings in Saxony, and filed.
